#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""robot-listen - watch one robot's traffic live, while you use it.

    robot-listen                 pick a robot, then listen
    robot-listen --robot Robot-A   skip the picker
    robot-listen --all-robots    every robot at once, still filtered
    robot-listen --everything    UNFILTERED, whole bridge (gated)
    robot-listen --check         verify prerequisites and exit

WHICH MODE
----------
    --robot        one robot. The default, and what you want when you are
                   watching a specific machine being used.
    --all-robots   all of them. Answers "is a single-robot filter hiding
                   traffic?" without recording anything belonging to anyone
                   else - it is still a filter, just a wider one.
    --everything   the whole bridge, including devices that are not ours.
                   Requires RL_WIDE_OK=i-have-authorisation, because that is a
                   decision for a person rather than a flag.

                   It automatically excludes the SSH session carrying the
                   capture. Without that the stream records itself: every
                   captured packet is sent back over that session, generating
                   more packets to capture. On the first unfiltered run the top
                   talker was our own capture.

WHAT THIS IS FOR, AND WHY IT IS NOT robot-round-up
---------------------------------------------------
round-up captures a controlled boot and analyses it afterwards. This is the
opposite shape: it shows traffic AS IT HAPPENS, so a person can do something to
the robot and watch what the network does a second later.

That makes it the first tool in this project aimed at a MODE rather than a
state - what changes when the cloud AI is actually used.

THE FEATURE THAT MATTERS IS NOT THE LIVE VIEW
---------------------------------------------
It is the MARK key. Type anything and press enter and it lands in the timeline,
timestamped to the same clock as the packets.

Day 6 established that a network record is not interpretable without an operator
record beside it - we could not say whether a silent robot was off or simply
quiet, because nobody had written down what was done and when. A tool that
watches traffic while a person operates the robot is exactly where that gap
opens, so the operator log is built in rather than left to a clipboard.

SCOPE
-----
Passive. Capture happens at the access point we administer, filtered to ONE
hardware address. Management queries and capture transport use SSH; robot discovery probes and DNS lookups are not generated. Names are
learned by watching the robot's own DNS answers go past - never by us asking.
"""

